                          %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/integration+of+differential+forms"} of differential forms
                        %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                          %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/push-forward+in+generalized+cohomology"}> push-forward in generalized cohomology
                          \/
                          %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fiber+integration+in+differential+cohomology"} in differential cohomology
                  %h2#analytic_integration Analytic integration
                  %p
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/integral+calculus"} integral calculus
                  %p
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Riemann+integration"}> Riemann integration
                    ,
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lebesgue+integration"} Lebesgue integration
                  %p
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/line+integral"}> line integral
                    \/
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/contour+integration"} contour integration
                  %p
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/integration+of+differential+forms"} integration of differential forms
                  %p
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/integration+over+supermanifolds"}> integration over supermanifolds
                    ,
                    = succeed "," do
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Berezin+integral"} Berezin integral
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fermionic+path+integral"} fermionic path integral
                  %p
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kontsevich+integral"}> Kontsevich integral
                    ,
                    = succeed "," do
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Selberg+integral"} Selberg integral
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/elliptic+Selberg+integral"} elliptic Selberg integral
                  %h2#cohomological_integration Cohomological integration
                  %p
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/integration+in+ordinary+differential+cohomology"} integration in ordinary differential cohomology
                  %p
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/integration+in+differential+K-theory"} integration in differential K-theory
                  %p
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Riemann-Roch+theorem"} Riemann-Roch theorem
                  %h2#variants Variants
                  %p
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+integration"} Lie integration
                  %p
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/path+integral"} path integral
                  %p
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Batalin-Vilkovisky+integral"} Batalin-Vilkovisky integral
          %h1#contents Contents
          .maruku_toc
            %ul
              %li
                %a{:href => "#idea"} Idea
              %ul
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#no_topdegree_forms_in_supergeometry"} No top-degree forms in supergeometry
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#solution"} Solution
              %li
                %a{:href => "#related_concepts"} Related concepts
              %li
                %a{:href => "#references"} References
          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            There are different ways to define a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/volume+form"} differential volume element
            on a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+manifold"} smooth manifold
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergeometry"} supergeometry
            = succeed "s," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+form"} differential form
            %em not
            carry over to
            = succeed "s." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supermanifold"} supermanifold
          %h3#no_topdegree_forms_in_supergeometry No top-degree forms in supergeometry
          %p
            In supergeometry the notion of
            %em top-degree form
            does not in general make sense, since there are no top-degree wedge powers of “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/superdifferential+form"}> odd 1-forms
            ”: if for instance
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi θ
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \theta_1
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi θ
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \theta_2
            are odd functions on some
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/super+Cartesian+space"} super Cartesian space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mrow
                      %mi p
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %mi q
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^{p|q}
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi d
                  %msub
                    %mi θ
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d \theta_1
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi d
                  %msub
                    %mi θ
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d \theta_2
            are their
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+1-forms"} differential 1-forms
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi d
                  %msub
                    %mi θ
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ∧
                  %mi d
                  %msub
                    %mi θ
                    %mn 2
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} +
                  %mi d
                  %msub
                    %mi θ
                    %mn 2
                  %mo ∧
                  %mi d
                  %msub
                    %mi θ
                    %mn 1
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  d\theta_1 \wedge d \theta_2 = +
                  d\theta_2 \wedge d \theta_1
                  \\,.
          %p
            Notice the plus sign on the right, which is the product of one minus sign for interchanging
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi θ
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \theta_1
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi θ
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \theta_2
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/signs+in+supergeometry"} signs in supergeometry
            for more on this.
          %p
            Accordingly, the wedge product of the differential of an odd function
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi θ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \theta
            with itself does not in general vanish:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi d
                  %mi θ
                  %mo ∧
                  %mi d
                  %mi θ
                  %mo =
                  %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⇔
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi θ
                  %mo =
                  %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  (d \theta \wedge d\theta = 0)
                  \\Leftrightarrow
                  (\theta = 0)
                  \\,.
          %p
            On the cartesian supermanifold
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %mi m
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^{n|m}
            with canonical even coordinate functions
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %msup
                    %mi x
                    %mi i
                  %msubsup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %mn 1
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{x^i\}_1^n
            and canonical odd coordinate functions
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %msup
                    %mi θ
                    %mi j
                  %msubsup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %mn 1
                    %mi m
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{\theta^j\}_1^m
            the differential form which one would want to regard as the canonical
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/volume+form"} volume form
            is
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ω
                  %mo :
                  %mo =
                  %mi d
                  %msup
                    %mi x
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ∧
                  %mi ⋯
                  %mo ∧
                  %mi d
                  %msup
                    %mi x
                    %mi n
                  %mo ∧
                  %mi d
                  %msup
                    %mi θ
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ∧
                  %mi ⋯
                  %mo ∧
                  %mi d
                  %msup
                    %mi θ
                    %mi m
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\omega :=
                  d x^1 \wedge \cdots \wedge d x^n \wedge
                  d\theta^1 \wedge \cdots \wedge d\theta^m
                  \\,.
          %p
            Due to the above, this is not a
            %em top
            form, since for instance
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ω
                  %mo ∧
                  %mi d
                  %msup
                    %mi θ
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ≠
                  %mn 0
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\omega \wedge d\theta^1 \neq 0
                  \\,.
          %p
            But this example also indicates the solution: apparently for integration it is not really essential that a form is a top power, what is rather essential is that it is, locally, the wedge product of a
            %em basis
            of 1-forms. This perspective then does lead to a sensible definition of volume forms (and more generally “integrable forms”) on supermanifolds, described below.
          %h3#solution Solution
          %p
            Therefore the naïve identification of differential volume measures with top degree forms has to be refined. The idea is to characterize a volume form by other means, in particular as an equivalence class of choices of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bases"} bases
            for the space of 1-forms, and then to define
            %strong integrable forms
            to be pairs consisting of such a generalized volume form and a
            = succeed ":" do
              %strong multivector
          %p
            The definition of integration of integrable forms in supergeometry in terms of multivector fields leads, in the case that the supermanifold in an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/NQ-supermanifold"} NQ-supermanifold
            to the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/BV+theory"} BV formalism
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/integration"} integration
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Berezin+integral"} Berezin integral
          %h2#references References
          %ul
            %li#DeligneMorgan99
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Pierre+Deligne"}> Pierre Deligne
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/John+Morgan"} John Morgan
                = succeed "," do
                  %em
                    Notes on Supersymmetry (following
                    = succeed ")" do
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Joseph+Bernstein"} Joseph Bernstein
                = succeed "," do
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Quantum+Fields+and+Strings"}> Quantum Fields and Strings
                    , A course for mathematicians
                = succeed "," do
                  %strong 1
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "https://bookstore.ams.org/qft-1-2-s"} ISBN:978-0-8218-2014-8
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "http://www.math.ias.edu/qft"} web version
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/files/DeligneMorgan-NotesOnSusy.pdf", :title => "pdf"} pdf
            %li{:id => "Mirković04"}
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ivan+Mirkovi%C4%87"}> Ivan Mirković
                , Sec 4-6 in:
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Notes on Super Math
                = succeed "," do
                  %em
                    %a{:href => "https://people.math.umass.edu/~mirkovic/0.SEMINARS/1.QFT/Fall.04.html"} Quantum Field Theory Seminar
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "https://people.math.umass.edu/~mirkovic/0.SEMINARS/1.QFT/1.SuperMath/8.pdf"} pdf
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/files/Mirkovic-NotesOnSupermathematics.pdf", :title => "pdf"} pdf
          %p Exposition:
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Urs+Schreiber"}> Urs Schreiber
              ,
              %em
                %a{:href => "http://www.math.uni-hamburg.de/home/schreiber/sin.pdf"}> Integration over supermanifolds
                \&amp;
          %p
            A general abstract discussion in terms of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/D-module"} D-module
            theory:
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Fr%C3%A9d%C3%A9ric+Paugam"}> Frédéric Paugam
              ,
              %em Homotopical Poisson Reduction of gauge theories
              (
              %a{:href => "http://people.math.jussieu.fr/~fpaugam/documents/homotopical-poisson-reduction-of-gauge-theories.pdf"}> pdf
              )
          %p
            Geometric discussion of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/picture+number"} picture number
            appearing in the context of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/integration+over+supermanifolds"} integration over supermanifolds
            (and originally seen in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantization"} quantization
            of the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/NSR+superstring"} NSR superstring
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/superstring+field+theory"} superstring field theory
          %ul
            %li#Belopolsky97b
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alexander+Belopolsky"}> Alexander Belopolsky
              ,
              %em Picture changing operators in supergeometry and superstring theory
              (
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/hep-th/9706033"}> arXiv:hep-th/9706033
              )
          %p and further amplified in
          %ul
            %li#Witten12
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Edward+Witten"}> Edward Witten
              , appendix D of
              %em Notes On Super Riemann Surfaces And Their Moduli
              (
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1209.2459"}> arXiv:1209.2459
              )
          %p
            In this perspective picture number is an extra grading on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+forms+on+supermanifolds"} differential forms on supermanifolds
            induced from a choice of
            %em integral top-form
            needed to define
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/integration+over+supermanifolds"} integration over supermanifolds
          %ul
            %li#CatenacciGrassiNoja18
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Roberto+Catenacci"}> Roberto Catenacci
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Pietro+Grassi"} Pietro Grassi
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Simone+Noja"} Simone Noja
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Superstring Field Theory, Superforms and Supergeometry
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1807.09563"} arXiv:1807.09563
              %p
                (with an eye towards
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/superstring+field+theory"} superstring field theory
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/C.+A.+Cremonini"}> C. A. Cremonini
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Pietro+Grassi"} Pietro Grassi
                %em Pictures from Super Chern-Simons Theory
                (
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1907.07152"}> arXiv:1907.07152
                )
              %p
                (with an eye towards
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/super+Chern-Simons+theory"} super Chern-Simons theory
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/C.+A.+Cremonini"}> C. A. Cremonini
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Pietro+Grassi"} Pietro Grassi
                %em Supersymmetric Wilson Loops via Integral Forms
                (
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2003.01729"}> arXiv:2003.01729
                )
              %p
                (for super-
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Wilson+lines"}> Wilson lines
                )
          %p Review:
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Pietro+Grassi"}> Pietro Grassi
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Integral Forms and Applications
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "https://agenda.infn.it/event/8823/attachments/55101/64989/Grassi-SL.pdf"} pdf
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/files/GrassiIntegralForms2015.pdf", :title => "pdf"} pdf
          %p See also:
          %ul
            %li
              Sergio L. Cacciatori, Simone Noja, Riccardo Re,
              %em The Unifying Double Complex on Supermanifolds
              (
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2004.10906"}> arXiv:2004.10906
              )
